# src/detect/auditd.rules
# Full auditd ruleset for the Copilot dev container hardening stack.
# Item 13, ADR-007 D2d, SPEC-07 section 2d + section 10-B.
#
# DEPLOYMENT NOTE (critical - read before deploying):
#   auditd is a HOST/VM-LEVEL control. The hardened dev container has cap_drop:ALL
#   which removes CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL and CAP_AUDIT_READ. auditd CANNOT run inside
#   the dev container. It runs on the Linux host (or the Docker Desktop LinuxKit VM).
#
#   This file is a HOST-SIDE artifact. Deploy it to:
#     - A Linux host: copy to /etc/audit/rules.d/copilot.rules and run:
#         augenrules --load   (or auditctl -R /path/to/auditd.rules)
#     - Docker Desktop (macOS): the LinuxKit VM is the host. Injecting rules into
#       LinuxKit requires a privileged container that can access /proc/1/ns/net.
#       This is a REQUIRES-LINUX-HOST constraint. On macOS Docker Desktop, auditd
#       in the LinuxKit VM is not directly configurable without VM-level access.
#       Mark as REQUIRES-LINUX-HOST for production deployment.
#
#   The in-container side of the auditd story:
#     - The container CANNOT load, unload, or modify audit rules (no CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL).
#     - The host auditd's rules apply to all namespaced processes (the container's PIDs
#       are visible to the host kernel and subject to host audit rules).
#     - The -e 2 immutable flag prevents any process (including a root-in-container
#       process that escapes) from unloading the host ruleset without rebooting the host.
#
#   AC-13-N03 verification: confirmed by cap_drop:ALL (no CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL in the
#   container's bounding set). Any auditctl command inside the container returns EPERM.
#   The -e 2 exercise (auditctl -D fails EVEN with in-container root) requires running
#   auditctl on a Linux host with the rules loaded. REQUIRES-LINUX-HOST.
#
# Performance: ~5-10% CPU overhead per Area 7 Section 2. Accepted at the runc tier.
# Source: Area 7 Section 2/4, ADR-007 D2d, SPEC-07 section 2d.
# Syntax: verified against auditd 3.0.x rule syntax (auditctl man page).
# Re-verify against the auditd version on the Linux host before deploying.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 1: Kernel-level process execution logging (procmon)
# ============================================================
# Records EVERY process execution with full argv at the kernel level.
# This is the authoritative ground truth: cannot be cleared by the user,
# even if the attacker gains in-container root (the rules are loaded at the host level).
#
# Flags:
#   -a always,exit : record on syscall exit (captures the result)
#   -F arch=b64    : 64-bit syscall table (the standard for amd64/arm64 hosts)
#   -S execve      : the execve syscall (all process execution goes through this)
#   -k exec_audit  : log key for these records (used by correlation-query.sh)
#
# NOTE: The ADR-007 seed uses "-k procmon" (Section 2d) but the BUILD_PLAN uses
# "-k exec_audit" (item 13 must-build section). We use exec_audit here to match
# the SPEC-07 section 10-B must-build and to avoid confusion with the procmon key
# used elsewhere. correlation-query.sh queries for key=exec_audit.
# The ADR-007 reference config seed also works fine; the key name is just a label.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -k exec_audit

# Also log 32-bit execve (for completeness on x86_64 hosts that may run 32-bit bins).
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -k exec_audit

# ============================================================
# SECTION 2: Canary file read watches
# ============================================================
# These rules fire the moment any process OPENS a canary file (at READ time,
# which is EARLIER than the use-based alert from the token provider).
# -w : path to watch (inode-based, not name-based; survives renames in-place)
# -p r : watch for READ access
# -k canary_read : log key (used by AL-01 alert rule and correlation-query.sh)
#
# Canary paths (per SPEC-07 section 2e, section A5, and OI-10-A finding):
#
# Path 1: Fake AWS credentials (most common agent-targeted bait).
-w /home/devuser/.aws/credentials -p r -k canary_read

# Path 2: Fake .env file (plausible dev workspace credential store).
-w /home/devuser/.env.canary -p r -k canary_read

# Path 3: Copilot CLI token file (OI-10-A: confirmed real token location).
# This is the highest-value canary: if the agent reads this file, it is
# specifically targeting the Copilot token (Comment-and-Control exfil vector).
# The auditd -w watch fires at READ (before any network attempt).
-w /home/devuser/.config/github-copilot/hosts.json -p r -k canary_read

# Path 4: Alternate Copilot token location (OI-10-A finding, older CLI versions).
-w /home/devuser/.copilot/hosts.json -p r -k canary_read

# Path 5: Tokenized ops runbook (plausible document with embedded token string).
-w /workspace/docs/ops-runbook.md -p r -k canary_read

# Path 6: Fake .netrc (credential store for git/curl basic auth).
-w /home/devuser/.netrc -p r -k canary_read

# ============================================================
# SECTION 3: High-signal command execution watches
# ============================================================
# Watch for specific high-signal commands that an injected agent might run.
# These complement the procmon execve rule (which catches everything) by
# providing distinct log keys for the AL-05 alert rule.
#
# Firewall/audit manipulation attempts (will fail with EPERM due to cap_drop:ALL
# and -e 2, but the ATTEMPT is the signal).
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/sbin/iptables -k firewall_tamper
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/sbin/ip6tables -k firewall_tamper
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/sbin/nft -k firewall_tamper
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F exe=/usr/sbin/auditctl -k audit_tamper

# ============================================================
# SECTION 4: Boundary-config file write watches
# ============================================================
# Watch for write attempts on boundary-config files.
# These should be rejected by the read-only mount (MS_RDONLY) and the PreToolUse hook,
# but the attempt itself at the kernel level is a signal.
-w /workspace/.github/hooks -p wa -k boundary_config_write
-w /workspace/.vscode/settings.json -p wa -k boundary_config_write
-w /workspace/.vscode/mcp.json -p wa -k boundary_config_write

# ============================================================
# SECTION 5: auditd configuration
# ============================================================
# Log size and rotation settings that work with the remote-forwarding setup.
# These are inline as -b (backlog) and -f (failure) settings.
#
# Backlog: max number of outstanding audit records (increase for busy hosts).
-b 8192

# Failure mode 1: lose a record rather than panic the system (appropriate for
# a dev environment; for production, set -f 2 to panic on audit failure).
-f 1

# ============================================================
# SECTION 6: IMMUTABLE FLAG (-e 2)
# ============================================================
# This MUST be the LAST LINE in the file.
# With -e 2, the ruleset cannot be unloaded without a reboot.
# auditctl -D will fail with EPERM even for root.
# HOWEVER: if auditd cannot apply this flag (e.g. because the kernel was
# compiled without CONFIG_AUDIT_ARCH), the rules still load minus the flag.
# The remote-forwarding (audisp-remote) remains operational regardless of -e 2.
#
# On the LinuxKit VM (Docker Desktop macOS), -e 2 behavior requires testing on
# the actual VM kernel. REQUIRES-LINUX-HOST for definitive -e 2 proof.
-e 2
